 "standard_text": "solve one-variable linear equations with natural number solutions; verify the solution by substitution",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 7 maths worksheets on solving linear equations for the Australian Curriculum (AC9M7A03): one-step and two-step equations, brackets, checking solutions by substitution, and forming equations from problems. 100 questions with an answer key.",
 "key_big": "Do the same to both sides. Undo + and − first, then × and ÷.",
 "key_sub": "Check your answer by substituting it back into the equation.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>An equation is like a balance: whatever you do to one side, do to the other. Use <b>inverse operations</b> to get the variable on its own: + and − undo each other, × and ÷ undo each other.</p><p>For a two-step equation, undo the + or − first, then the × or ÷. <b>Verify</b> the solution by substituting it back into the equation.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve 4<i>x</i> + 7 = 31.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Subtract 7: 4<i>x</i> = 24.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Divide by 4: <i>x</i> = 6.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Check: 4 × 6 + 7 = 31. ✓</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 6</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve 3(<i>x</i> − 2) = 21.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Divide by 3: <i>x</i> − 2 = 7.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Add 2: <i>x</i> = 9.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 9</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve <i>n</i>/5 + 3 = 10.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Subtract 3: <i>n</i>/5 = 7.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Multiply by 5: <i>n</i> = 35.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>n</i> = 35</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Doing the same operation instead of the inverse: for <i>x</i> + 7 = 20, <i>x</i> = 13, not 27.</li><li>Dividing before subtracting: for 4<i>x</i> + 7 = 31, do 31 − 7 first, then ÷ 4.</li><li>Dividing the wrong way round: 6<i>x</i> = 30 gives <i>x</i> = 30 ÷ 6 = 5.</li><li>Not checking: put your answer back into the equation to see if it works.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "How do you solve a two-step equation?",
   "a": "Undo the addition or subtraction first, then the multiplication or division. For 4x + 7 = 31: 4x = 24, so x = 6."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I solve an equation with a bracket?",
   "a": "Divide both sides by the number outside the bracket, or expand it first. 3(x − 2) = 21 gives x − 2 = 7, so x = 9."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I verify a solution?",
   "a": "Substitute it back into the equation. If x = 6 in 4x + 7, then 24 + 7 = 31, so it is correct."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I turn a word problem into an equation?",
   "a": "Choose a letter for the unknown, write each part of the story as an expression, then set it equal to the total."
  }
 ],
